course: dictionary
lesson: 12590
title: Disbelieve
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To disbelieve something means you do not think it is true. You refuse to believe it. «Ravi heard the story about the flying cat, but he disbelieved every word.» The word «but» is a clue: Ravi heard the story, but he did not think it was true.
explain: Look at the start of the word. «Dis» often means «not», as in dislike and disagree. So «disbelieve» is like «not believe». «Mia told her friends she had met a dragon, and they disbelieved her, so they just laughed.» Their laughing shows they did not think it was true.
explain: Try swapping in another phrase. If «refuse to believe» fits the sentence and still makes sense, you have found the right meaning. Always check your meaning in the sentence.
fact: «Disbelieve» means to not believe that something is true.
fact: «Disbelieve» can also mean to not have faith in something, especially God. This is an older, formal use.
fact: In the Bible, Thomas disbelieved that Jesus had risen until he saw him. That is why he is called «Doubting Thomas».
fact: The forms are disbelieve, disbelieves, disbelieved and disbelieving.
fact: A good check is to swap in «refuse to believe» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The sailor disbelieved the warning about the storm.» What does disbelieved mean? | *Did not believe it was true | Forgot it on the boat | Believed it completely | Shouted loudly at it
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to disbelieve? | *Refuse to believe | Trust | Forget | Dance
exercise: «Tom heard the tall tale, but his sister disbelieved every word.» Find and copy the word that means did not believe. | *disbelieved | tale | word | heard
test: «The teacher disbelieved Sam's story about the lost dog.» What does disbelieved mean here? | Wrote it down in a book | Lost the dog | *Did not believe it was true | Believed it and cheered
test: Which sentence shows someone disbelieving a story? | The crowd went home early | *The crowd laughed and said, "That can't be true!" | The crowd sang a happy song | The crowd clapped and asked for more
exam: «Lily's friends disbelieved her tall tale about the talking goat.» Why did they disbelieve her? | They thought it was the best story ever | They had seen the goat too | *They thought it was not true | They wanted her to tell it again
